3.4 The logistic map
The logistic map is the simplest discrete-time model that produces both stable dynamics and chaos:
\[
N_{t+1} = r \, N_t (1 - N_t)
\]
where \(N_t \in [0, 1]\) is the population scaled by its maximum and \(r \in [0, 4]\) is the growth parameter. For \(r &lt; 1\) the population collapses to zero; for \(1 &lt; r &lt; 3\) it converges to a stable equilibrium \(N^* = 1 - 1/r\); beyond \(r = 3\) the equilibrium destabilises through a cascade of period-doubling bifurcations that culminates in deterministic chaos near \(r \approx 3.57\).

viewof r_val  = Inputs.range([1, 4],       {step: 0.01, value: 2,   label: "r"})
viewof n0_val = Inputs.range([0.01, 0.99], {step: 0.01, value: 0.5, label: "N₀"})

bif_arr = transpose(bif_grid)

n_ts = 100
ts_data = {
  const pts = [{t: 0, N: n0_val}]
  let N = n0_val
  for (let t = 1; t &lt;= n_ts; t++) {
    N = r_val * N * (1 - N)
    pts.push({t, N})
  }
  return pts
}

ts_plot = Plot.plot({
  width: 290, height: 250,
  x: {label: "t"},
  y: {domain: [0, 1], label: "Nₜ"},
  title: `N₀ = ${n0_val.toFixed(2)},  r = ${r_val.toFixed(2)}`,
  marks: [
    Plot.line(ts_data, {x: "t", y: "N", stroke: "#2980b9", strokeWidth: 1.2}),
    Plot.dot(ts_data,  {x: "t", y: "N", r: 1.2, fill: "#2980b9"})
  ]
})

bif_plot = Plot.plot({
  width: 410, height: 250,
  x: {domain: [1, 4], label: "r", ticks: [1, 2, 3, 4]},
  y: {domain: [0, 1], label: "Nₜ (attractor)"},
  title: "Bifurcation diagram",
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(bif_arr,   {x: "r", y: "N", r: 0.5, fill: "#666", opacity: 0.25}),
    Plot.ruleX([r_val], {stroke: "#e74c3c", strokeWidth: 2})
  ]
})

html`&lt;div style="display:flex; gap:16px; align-items:flex-start; flex-wrap:wrap"&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;${ts_plot}&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div&gt;${bif_plot}&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;`
